  "Question": "For values of \\(x\\) less than \\(1\\) but greater than \\(-4,\\) the expression\n\n\\[ \\frac{x^2-2x+2}{2x-2} \\]\n\nhas:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "no maximum or minimum value",
    "B": "a minimum value of \\(1\\)",
    "C": "a maximum value of \\(1\\)",
    "D": "a minimum value of \\(-1\\)",
    "E": "a maximum value of \\(-1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "To test the suggested bound \\(-1\\), add 1 to the expression. Write\n\\[\nF(x)=\\frac{x^2-2x+2}{2x-2}.\n\\]\nFor every allowed \\(x\\), the denominator \\(2x-2\\) is negative and nonzero. Thus\n\\[\nF(x)+1=\\frac{x^2}{2x-2}\\le0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(x^2\\ge0\\). Consequently \\(F(x)\\le-1\\), with equality exactly when \\(x=0\\). Since \\(-4<0<1\\), this bound is attained.\n\nThere is no minimum. For \\(0<t<1\\), the allowed input \\(x=1-t\\) gives\n\\[\nF(1-t)=-\\frac{t+1/t}{2}<-\\frac1{2t}.\n\\]\nTaking positive \\(t\\) sufficiently small makes these values arbitrarily negative.\n\nThe expression therefore has maximum value \\(\\boxed{-1}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
